  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased capacities for entrepreneurship development through sustainable enterprises, competitiveness and productive growth for youth and women</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Entrepreneurship remains a critical pathway to employment creation for youth and women in Zimbabwe, particularly in an economy characterised by limited formal employment opportunities and high informality. However, much of this entrepreneurship is necessity-driven, concentrated in low-productivity activities with constrained access to finance, markets, technology, technical and business skills. 

In line with the National Development Strategy 2 (NDS2) priorities on SME development, private sector growth and formalisation, there is a need to strengthen an enabling ecosystem that promotes opportunity-driven entrepreneurship, innovation, productivity and enterprise resilience. The ILO supports integrated interventions that foster the growth and sustainability of micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs), including social and solidarity economy (SSE) enterprises. This includes deploying the ILO suite of enterprise and skills development tools like SIYB, EYB/GYB, TREE, WISE, SURE to promote job creation, youth and women economic empowerment in key value chains including horticulture, diary processing and apiculture.

The support also seeks to build local capacities to deliver training and support to strengthen regulatory and institutional frameworks and improving the overall business environment to reduce barriers to enterprise growth and formalisation. Targeted efforts are also supported to facilitate the transition to formality for enterprise and workers through policy reforms, incentives and tailored advisory services.

ILO support further focuses on strengthening institutional capacity for skills development, entrepreneurship promotion and enterprise support systems, ensuring alignment between training, labour market demand and sectoral opportunities, particularly in agriculture and emerging sectors.</narrative>
  </description>
